    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e0d0e407-afb1-5566-8339-b8704746dc94",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $P$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $P$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(P)=\\frac{3}{2}$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\sqrt{2} & 0 & - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\sqrt{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\sqrt{2} & 0 & - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\sqrt{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(- \\sqrt{2})\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+(- \\frac{1}{2})\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(- \\sqrt{2})(\\sqrt{2}+0)+0+(- \\frac{1}{2})(0+1) \\\\  & =(- \\sqrt{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+(- \\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=- \\frac{5}{2}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
