      ":question/id": "17f46b82-1fc1-525b-97e4-b6485eaab46b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(N)=12 \\sqrt{3}$\n- **III.** $det(N)=0$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 \\sqrt{3} & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 \\sqrt{3} & -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 \\sqrt{3} & -2\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 3 \\sqrt{3} & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(4-6)+0+\\frac{1}{3}(0+6 \\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6 \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =- 2 \\sqrt{3}+2 \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
